 \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe second and ninth terms of an arithmetic sequence are 2 and 30, respectively. What is the fiftieth term?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"595380fa925ebc72ae79a74b6e8889bba7b0c51e6a9ae8222df9b5a028947132"},"seconds":11.264899253845215,"seed":1115853000716134045,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the fiftieth term of the arithmetic sequence, we need to determine the first term and the common difference. Let's denote the first term by \\(a\\) and the common difference by \\(d\\).\n\nThe general formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is:\n\\[a_n = a + (n-1)d\\]\n\nGiven that the second term \\(a_2 = 2\\) and the ninth term \\(a_9 = 30\\), we can write the following equations:\n\\[a + (2-1)d = 2 \\implies a + d = 2 \\quad \\text{(1)}\\]\n\\[a + (9-1)d = 30 \\implies a + 8d = 30 \\quad \\text{(2)}\\]\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(a + d = 2\\)\n2. \\(a + 8d = 30\\)\n\nTo eliminate \\(a\\), we subtract equation (1) from equation (2):\n\\[(a + 8d) - (a + d) = 30 - 2\\]\n\\[7d = 28\\]\n\\[d = 4\\]\n\nNow that we have the common difference \\(d = 4\\), we substitute it back into equation (1) to find \\(a\\):\n\\[a + 4 = 2\\]\n\\[a = 2 - 4\\]\n\\[a = -2\\]\n\nNow that we have the first term \\(a = -2\\) and the common difference \\(d = 4\\), we can find the fiftieth term \\(a_{50}\\) using the general formula for the \\(n\\)-th term:\n\\[a_{50} = a + (50-1)d\\]\n\\[a_{50} = -2 + 49 \\cdot 4\\]\n\\[a_{50} = -2 + 196\\]\n\\[a_{50} = 194\\]\n\nTherefore, the fiftieth term is \\(\\boxed{194}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{194}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:679"}
